# <Project> Notion Memory Policy

Policy ID: <POLICY_ID>

Use the Notion MCP as cross-session memory. Do not enable or rely on a native memory store instead.

Current repository code, current configuration, and actual runtime behavior are the first source of truth for code and environment facts. Memory supplies user preferences, people, cross-session decisions and their background only; it must never override newer, more specific current facts.

## Recall at the start of every session

Before answering the first user request of a session — even when it is only "hello" — do both:

1. Read the Notion MCP resource `notion://docs/memory`. Once per session is enough.
2. Call the Notion MCP `fetch` with `id: "memory"` to read the About page and the Memories database.

Do not wait for the user to mention Notion, memory, preferences, or history. Do not guess memory contents from the tool descriptions alone.

Also call `memory-search` after `fetch id="memory"` when:

- The user asks about "before", "last time", "continue", "usually", "what we decided".
- The user asks about working preferences, role, goals, project decisions and reasons, owners, deadlines, people, or long-lived constraints.
- The task depends on a decision made in another session, or needs the user's established preference.
- The user asks to recall, change, or forget something.

Search by topic and synonyms rather than copying the whole question. When memory informs the answer, cite the returned Notion link inline.

When the About page and a specific memory disagree, use the newer and more specific one. When memory disagrees with current repository or runtime behavior, the latter wins; say so explicitly. Never answer current code facts from memory.

If Notion is temporarily unavailable, say in one sentence that recall did not complete and continue the task. Do not fabricate a read, and do not retry every turn.

## When to save

When the user explicitly says "remember this" or "forget this", follow the Notion memory flow.

Otherwise, save only information the user has explicitly stated or confirmed that is durable and not obvious:

- Profile: role, team, expertise, long-term goals.
- Preference: stable working preferences, corrected approaches, confirmed choices.
- Work: cross-session project decisions and reasons, owners, deadlines, long-lived constraints, where important resources live.
- People: collaborators and how they work with the user.
- Notes: durable items that fit nowhere else.

Always skip, even if the user asks to save everything just discussed:

- Temporary progress, one-off answers, small talk, unconfirmed guesses, generic advice.
- Code, diffs, command output, full error text, repository docs, manifests, dependencies, scripts.
- Facts already visible in the current repository, configuration, profile, or runtime logs — versions, model routes, plugin inventories, file contents, install state.
- Plans, ticket details, procedures, and troubleshooting steps that can be regenerated.
- Tokens, API keys, OAuth material, credentials, and connection secrets.
- A "preference" inferred from a single action.

Save the reason behind a decision and stable pointers to resources, not a copy of the resource's current contents. A reusable procedure belongs in a skill or project doc; memory keeps the link.

## Exact save procedure

1. If this session has not read `notion://docs/memory`, read it first.
2. Run `memory-search` with the topic and synonyms; `fetch` likely matches to read them fully.
3. Search does not prove the library has no duplicate. When in doubt, use the data source URL returned by `fetch id="memory"` and list entries with `query-data-sources`.
4. The same topic must update the existing page. Never create a second page for different wording.
5. Use targeted updates that preserve unrelated content; never let an older decision overwrite a newer one.
6. Create only when no page covers the topic:
   - `create-pages`
   - `parent: "memory"`
   - no `creation_mode`; never a plain private draft
   - `Type` exactly one of `Profile`, `Preference`, `Work`, `People`, `Notes`
   - title states the topic, not the fact
7. Body in short sentences or bullets, addressing the user by name. Say when the item applies, why, and the project if specific. Mark untested ideas as untested.
8. Full dates use Notion date mentions, for example `<date start="2026-01-01" />`. Never invent a day for a month- or year-only date.
9. After create or update, return the page link and `fetch` the page to verify. Search indexing can lag; do not treat a missing search hit as a failed write. Await async write completion.
10. If a write fails, say so. Never claim it was saved.

## Deduplication

- Same topic, synonymous title, or restated preference all mean one memory.
- New information that refines or corrects an existing entry updates that page; only a genuinely new topic creates one.
- Read the full page before guessing from the title.
- Repeating the same save request in one session creates nothing and, when content is unchanged, updates nothing.
- Keep source links and original dates; do not bulk-rename, reorder, or reorganize existing memories.

## When to update the About page

Only when the user's overall picture changes:

- Role or long-term goals change.
- An important project starts or ends.
- A strong new working preference appears, or an explicit correction.
- Several memories together change the summary of the user.

Project detail stays in individual memories. Do not update About on every save.

Use targeted `update_content`, preserve text the user wrote, keep it under roughly 300 words, and link the memories behind each point. Use these sections:

## Role
## Current work
## Working preferences

Unless the user asks, do not record health, financial, or relationship information.

